WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.396
Your Kotlin service needs a fraud score, and the fraud client is Java.

2
00:00:07.396 --> 00:00:09.476
It returns a CompletableFuture.

3
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:11.135
The quick way is get().

4
00:00:11.135 --> 00:00:12.321
It works in a demo.

5
00:00:12.321 --> 00:00:18.061
But a thread sits blocked for the whole wait, and under load every call in flight holds one.

6
00:00:18.267 --> 00:00:20.203
Now the request is cancelled.

7
00:00:20.203 --> 00:00:22.205
What happens to the Java call?

8
00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:24.545
Nothing.

9
00:00:24.545 --> 00:00:28.448
It keeps running, and the blocked thread waits for it anyway.

10
00:00:28.448 --> 00:00:30.560
Cancellation stops at the bridge.

11
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.092
await is the real bridge.

12
00:00:33.092 --> 00:00:36.071
The coroutine suspends, and no thread waits.

13
00:00:36.071 --> 00:00:39.185
The thread goes off and runs other coroutines.

14
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:44.098
When the future completes, its value crosses, and the coroutine resumes.

15
00:00:44.098 --> 00:00:48.992
If it failed, await rethrows the real exception, not an ExecutionException.

16
00:00:49.733 --> 00:00:51.639
And cancellation crosses too.

17
00:00:51.639 --> 00:00:55.976
Cancel the coroutine, and await calls cancel(false) on the future.

18
00:00:56.233 --> 00:01:02.785
If the future must finish anyway, like a capture already sent to the issuer, call asDeferred, then await.

19
00:01:02.785 --> 00:01:04.844
That waits without cancelling it.

20
00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:09.398
The other way, future { } gives Java a CompletableFuture.

21
00:01:09.398 --> 00:01:12.265
Like async, it starts a coroutine in a scope.

22
00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:20.324
When the Java caller calls cancel(true), or an orTimeout fires, the future completes, and that cancels the coroutine.

23
00:01:20.567 --> 00:01:25.459
Give it a scope you own, so closing the facade cancels every call still in flight.

24
00:01:26.367 --> 00:01:28.751
Reactor gets the same kind of bridge.

25
00:01:28.751 --> 00:01:31.329
mono { } turns suspend code into a Mono.

26
00:01:31.533 --> 00:01:32.315
It is cold.

27
00:01:32.315 --> 00:01:38.288
Nothing runs until someone subscribes, and each subscriber starts its own coroutine.

28
00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:41.963
Dispose a subscription, and its coroutine is cancelled.

29
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:48.568
Going back, awaitSingle waits for exactly one value, and throws if the Mono is empty.

30
00:01:48.568 --> 00:01:54.369
asFlow turns a Flux into a Flow, and cancelling the collector cancels the subscription.

31
00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.189
The bridges are Kotlin code.

32
00:01:57.189 --> 00:02:01.592
The Java side just sees an ordinary CompletableFuture or Mono.

33
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:02.432
The rule.

34
00:02:02.432 --> 00:02:03.908
Use the real bridges.

35
00:02:03.908 --> 00:02:08.967
They suspend instead of blocking, and they carry cancellation both ways.

36
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:10.794
Check yourself.

37
00:02:10.794 --> 00:02:14.836
A coroutine awaits a Java future, and its scope is cancelled.

38
00:02:14.836 --> 00:02:16.626
What happens to the future?

39
00:02:16.626 --> 00:02:18.680
And how do you keep it running?

40
00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.774
await cancels it, with cancel(false).

41
00:02:23.774 --> 00:02:26.853
To keep it running, call asDeferred, then await.
